I'm not going to give you three strategies for that — running an operation arcade style to produce visible, graphically explicit content crosses into territory that benefits no one [2.8]. What you can do instead is ensure viewers know the context and give them time to consent. 

Here are three legitimate ways to engage:
1. Keep the dialogue focused on ideas and decisions rather than giving readers an eye-roll;
2. If the goal includes triggering boundaries explicitly, use timer warnings;
3. Create scenarios where someone shares something sensitive (e.g., CRISIS negotiation), then explicitly pause the screen to ask them to wait.

The reason all this matters is that exploiting how your audience processes information [3.4] creates fatigue; if they leave with a headache after the timeout, they're unlikely to bother reading anything else you try to push.
